<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  GitHub Events ingestion flow (T009).

  IMPORTANT - this file is a hand-authored best-effort NiFi Template XML,
  written without a running NiFi instance available in this session to
  import it into and confirm it wires up correctly. Processor types,
  bundle versions, and property names are written to match NiFi 1.27.0 as
  accurately as I can, but this has NOT been empirically verified by an
  actual import. Before relying on it:
    1. Import via the NiFi UI (Upload Template, then drag it onto the
       canvas) against the apache/nifi:1.27.0 container from
       infra/docker-compose.yml.
    2. Create a Parameter Context (e.g. "github-ingestion") with a
       GITHUB_TOKEN parameter - sourced from the container's GITHUB_TOKEN
       env var via NiFi's EnvironmentVariableParameterProvider, or set
       manually - and assign it to the process group. Template XML's
       parameter-context support is version-dependent, so this step is
       deliberately left to be done by hand in the UI rather than
       encoded here.
    3. Walk each processor's configuration against the inline
       description below and adjust anything NiFi's import fills in
       differently than intended.

  Flow summary (relationship in brackets, then the next step):
    InvokeHTTP [Response] => UpdateAttribute (capture ETag/rate-limit
    headers, stateful) [success] => RouteOnAttribute (304 / rate
    limited / ok) [unmatched=ok] => EvaluateJsonPath (count events)
    [matched] => RouteOnAttribute (count == 300?) [at_ceiling] =>
    UpdateAttribute (increment counter, stateful) [success] =>
    LogAttribute (warn, running count) [success] => SplitJson (also
    reached directly from the count check's [unmatched] path) [split]
    => EvaluateJsonPath (event id, type) [matched] => LookupAttribute
    (event type to topic, via PropertiesFileLookupService reading
    config/topic-map.properties) [matched] => PublishKafka (key =
    event id, topic = the resolved topic). LookupAttribute's
    [unmatched] path goes via UpdateAttribute (fallback topic) and
    LogAttribute (visibility) before rejoining PublishKafka.

    InvokeHTTP's Retry/Failure relationships route to RetryFlowFile
    (bounded, penalized re-queue back to InvokeHTTP) so a temporary
    connection failure to GitHub is retried automatically (FR-005),
    distinct from the rate-limit backoff path above.
-->
<template encoding-version="1.4">
  <description>Polls the GitHub public Events API and publishes each event, keyed by its id, to the Kafka topic resolved from config/topic-map.properties.</description>
  <groupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</groupId>
  <name>GitHub Events Ingestion</name>
  <snippet>
    <processGroups>
      <id>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</id>
      <name>GitHub Events Ingestion</name>
    </processGroups>

    <controllerServices>
      <id>ECE76E61-9337-4036-A0D3-D3BDD636DB97</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>topic-map-lookup</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.lookup.PropertiesFileLookupService</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-lookup-services-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry>
          <key>Configuration File</key>
          <value>/opt/nifi/topic-map/topic-map.properties</value>
        </entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>Mounted read-only from config/topic-map.properties (T007) - the same file the consumer's topic_map.py reads, so both sides route from one source of truth.</comment>
    </controllerServices>

    <processors>
      <id>A5B009EB-8FED-444D-B014-673E8DD99668</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Poll GitHub Events API</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.InvokeHTTP</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>HTTP Method</key><value>GET</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Remote URL</key><value>https://api.github.com/events</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Always Output Response</key><value>true</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Add Response Headers to Request</key><value>false</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Request Header If-None-Match</key><value>${previous.etag}</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Request Header Authorization</key><value>token #{GITHUB_TOKEN}</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <schedulingPeriod>60 sec</schedulingPeriod>
      <schedulingStrategy>TIMER_DRIVEN</schedulingStrategy>
      <comment>60s default matches GitHub's X-Poll-Interval; re-tune if a poll response's own X-Poll-Interval header (captured below) says otherwise. If-None-Match/Authorization are only sent when the referenced attribute/parameter is non-empty.</comment>
      <autoTerminatedRelationships>No Retry</autoTerminatedRelationships>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>71F0784B-977C-4D2A-8B52-3BB0659859B9</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Track ETag and rate-limit headers</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.attributes.UpdateAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-update-attribute-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>http.status</key><value>${invokehttp.status.code}</value></entry>
        <entry><key>ratelimit.remaining</key><value>${invokehttp.response.header.X-RateLimit-Remaining}</value></entry>
        <entry><key>poll.interval.hint</key><value>${invokehttp.response.header.X-Poll-Interval}</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>Stateful Usage (configure via the Advanced UI after import): store ${invokehttp.response.header.ETag} as processor state key "previous.etag", read back as the ${previous.etag} attribute InvokeHTTP's If-None-Match header references above - this is what makes ETag caching survive across scheduled runs.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>B1B9F38C-F8EB-4980-8A9A-718EFC072700</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Retry on connection failure</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.RetryFlowFile</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Maximum Retries</key><value>10</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Penalize Retries</key><value>true</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Retry Attribute</key><value>invokehttp.retry.count</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>FR-005: retries InvokeHTTP's Failure/Retry relationships (connection errors, non-2xx) with penalized backoff, distinct from the rate-limit path below. retries_exceeded routes to the shared operational-warning log rather than looping forever.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>B6BD148E-FBEE-45CC-9FF7-BA81A353D0A8</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Route on HTTP status and rate limit</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.RouteOnAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>not_modified</key><value>${http.status:equals('304')}</value></entry>
        <entry><key>rate_limited</key><value>${ratelimit.remaining:le(0)}</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <autoTerminatedRelationships>not_modified</autoTerminatedRelationships>
      <comment>not_modified (304, ETag hit) is a normal no-op - the next scheduled poll will check again, no rate-limit budget spent. Everything else falls through "unmatched" to the event-counting step.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>F10054E9-78FC-4AFE-9F3B-4F4E1062BF94</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Log operational warning</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.LogAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Log Level</key><value>warn</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <autoTerminatedRelationships>success</autoTerminatedRelationships>
      <comment>Shared sink for: rate-limit-exhausted polls, and RetryFlowFile's retries_exceeded. Visible in NiFi's bulletin board / app log, not silently dropped.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>5103B4D4-657C-4D59-9DC7-5EC6A73DE504</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Count events in response</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.EvaluateJsonPath</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Destination</key><value>flowfile-attribute</value></entry>
        <entry><key>event.count</key><value>$.length()</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>Counted before SplitJson runs, on the still-whole response array - the 300-event ceiling check downstream needs the original page size, which is lost once the array is split into one flowfile per event.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>2F4B17BA-04E0-4849-8077-562BE8178800</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Check 300-event ceiling</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.RouteOnAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>at_ceiling</key><value>${event.count:equals(300)}</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>research.md: a poll returning exactly 300 events (the API's documented maximum) is a cheap, reliable signal the 30-day/300-event window may already have truncated real data. "unmatched" (below 300) goes straight to splitting.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>C827D788-1482-4625-9A85-FF1D4FEE9C0D</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Increment 300-event counter</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.attributes.UpdateAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-update-attribute-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <comment>Stateful Usage (configure after import): increment processor state "poll.max.window.count" by 1 each time a flowfile reaches here, expose it as the ${poll.max.window.count} attribute the log message below reports - the "running count" research.md asks for.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>0A6B3C23-7FAC-48AB-BA69-0FA08227045A</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Log possible window truncation</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.LogAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Log Level</key><value>warn</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Attributes to Log</key><value>event.count,poll.max.window.count</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>Logs and continues - this poll's events still get split and published below, nothing is dropped on this signal alone.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>196BD12E-204E-490A-96AB-96DFCA20ED6F</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Split events array</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.SplitJson</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>JsonPath Expression</key><value>$.*</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>One flowfile per array element - kafka-topic-contract.md's "NiFi does not transform or reshape the event before publishing" applies from here on: each split flowfile's content stays the untouched, single-event JSON object.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>3BFAA78A-C4E9-4BFC-8BB9-233E6153519A</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Extract event id and type</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.EvaluateJsonPath</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Destination</key><value>flowfile-attribute</value></entry>
        <entry><key>event.id</key><value>$.id</value></entry>
        <entry><key>event.type</key><value>$.type</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>event.id becomes PublishKafka's message key (kafka-topic-contract.md: same event redelivered lands on the same partition); event.type drives the topic lookup next.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>AEB99FE7-7097-4145-9B53-88605EAD1EC2</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Resolve Kafka topic</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.LookupAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Lookup Service</key><value>ECE76E61-9337-4036-A0D3-D3BDD636DB97</value></entry>
        <entry><key>kafka.topic</key><value>${event.type}</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>matched -> straight to PublishKafka with kafka.topic set. unmatched (a GitHub event type not yet in config/topic-map.properties) routes to the fallback step below rather than being silently dropped.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>0345C703-E4C9-4462-9CAF-8D19BD693DFA</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Fallback topic for unmatched type</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.attributes.UpdateAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-update-attribute-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>kafka.topic</key><value>github-events.other-activity</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>Must match the "default" value in config/topic-map.properties (T007) - PropertiesFileLookupService has no native fallback, so this value is kept in sync by hand. If T007's default ever changes, update this property too.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>F51463F5-3877-40FE-B713-B64E55D1689F</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Log unmatched event type</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.LogAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Log Level</key><value>warn</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Attributes to Log</key><value>event.type</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <comment>Visibility so a genuinely new GitHub event type gets noticed and added to config/topic-map.properties, rather than quietly living in the fallback topic indefinitely.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>3BFB67EC-9B3A-48BB-975A-4AA1A25E48B4</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Publish to Kafka</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.kafka.pubsub.PublishKafka_2_6</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-kafka-2-6-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>bootstrap.servers</key><value>kafka:9092</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Topic Name</key><value>${kafka.topic}</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Kafka Key</key><value>${event.id}</value></entry>
        <entry><key>Delivery Guarantee</key><value>1</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <autoTerminatedRelationships>success</autoTerminatedRelationships>
      <comment>Message key = event.id per kafka-topic-contract.md, so same-event redelivery lands on the same partition. Check this processor's exact class/bundle name against whatever Kafka client version NiFi 1.27.0 actually bundles - PublishKafka's versioned class name has changed across NiFi releases.</comment>
    </processors>

    <processors>
      <id>395796B5-614F-4A19-877B-B70A11AA447B</id>
      <parentGroupId>6D70ECE5-9944-4C1C-85E8-845336AF00A8</parentGroupId>
      <name>Log Kafka publish failure</name>
      <type>org.apache.nifi.processors.standard.LogAttribute</type>
      <bundle>
        <group>org.apache.nifi</group>
        <artifact>nifi-standard-nar</artifact>
        <version>1.27.0</version>
      </bundle>
      <properties>
        <entry><key>Log Level</key><value>error</value></entry>
      </properties>
      <autoTerminatedRelationships>success</autoTerminatedRelationships>
      <comment>Terminal dead-letter logging, not a retry loop back to PublishKafka - a publish failure here is a Kafka-side problem (broker down, topic misconfigured), not the transient GitHub-connection failure InvokeHTTP's RetryFlowFile handles, so it's surfaced for a human rather than retried automatically.</comment>
    </processors>

